      "hints": [
        "First remove the secant and tangent denominators so the equation becomes a linear combination of $\\cos x$ and $\\sin x$.",
        "Multiplying by $\\cos x$ gives $4\\cos x-\\sin x=\\sqrt5$; compare this with $R\\cos(x+\\alpha)=R\\cos\\alpha\\cos x-R\\sin\\alpha\\sin x$.",
        "Thus use $R\\cos\\alpha=4$ and $R\\sin\\alpha=1$: square and add for exact $R$, then use their ratio for the acute angle $\\alpha$ to 2 decimal places."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Rearrange and obtain $4\\cos x-\\sin x=\\sqrt5$",
        "State $R=\\sqrt{17}$",
        "Use a trig formula to find $\\alpha$",
        "Obtain $\\alpha=14.04^\\circ$",
        "$R=\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\alpha=14.04^\\circ$."
      ],
